{
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Case Twelve.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.753,
   "pause": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "The Stopped Clock.",
   "start": 2.3529999999999998,
   "end": 3.569,
   "pause": 1.4
  },
  {
   "text": "The big clock on the wall of the Kettle and Gull is older than Agnes Bell's grandmother. On Monday morning it gave a tired little click and stopped quite still at ten to nine. Agnes sent for the clockmaker from town, but he could not come until Friday. So all week, the clock's hands pointed to ten to nine.",
   "start": 4.969,
   "end": 23.849,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"You'll have to tell the time by your stomachs,\" Agnes told her customers, and they all laughed.",
   "start": 24.699,
   "end": 29.989,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "On the shelf behind the counter, Agnes kept her collection of antique tea caddies. The finest of them was a little silver one shaped like an acorn. On Thursday at half past three she dusted it, and it was there. At half past four she noticed it was gone.",
   "start": 30.839,
   "end": 46.925,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Three people had been in the tea room on Thursday afternoon.",
   "start": 47.775,
   "end": 50.975,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Demelza Rowe had come in at a quarter past three to drop off some paintings, and left straight away. At half past three she was at the sea front with Captain Quill, and they stayed together all afternoon.",
   "start": 51.825,
   "end": 64.155,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Hedley Truscott had fixed a squeaky door hinge at three o'clock and gone home. His wife said he had been asleep in his armchair from twenty past three until supper time, snoring loud enough to rattle the windows.",
   "start": 65.005,
   "end": 78.104,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Prowse, who delivers the milk, had brought the afternoon crate.",
   "start": 78.954,
   "end": 82.794,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"I wasn't here anywhere near half past three,\" said Mr Prowse. \"I dropped the crate at the back door, popped my head in, and looked up at your clock. It said a quarter past three, so I knew I was running early. I was gone a couple of minutes later.\"",
   "start": 83.644,
   "end": 98.3,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Agnes looked up at her clock. It said ten to nine, as it had all week.",
   "start": 99.15,
   "end": 103.587,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Can you solve it? How does Agnes know Mr Prowse is not telling the truth?",
   "start": 104.687,
   "end": 108.975,
   "pause": 1.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Think about it.",
   "start": 110.175,
   "end": 111.199,
   "pause": 0.9
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
   "start": 112.099,
   "end": 117.326,
   "pause": 4.5
  },
  {
   "text": "The Solution.",
   "start": 121.826,
   "end": 122.85,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Prowse took the silver acorn caddy.",
   "start": 123.95,
   "end": 126.531,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "He said he had looked at the tea room clock and seen that it was a quarter past three. But the clock had been stopped at ten to nine since Monday. It could not have said a quarter past three on Thursday. Mr Prowse had made up the time to make it sound as if he had left before the caddy disappeared.",
   "start": 127.381,
   "end": 144.491,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Demelza had left before half past three and was with Captain Quill, and Hedley was asleep at home. Only Mr Prowse had been at the tea room after half past three.",
   "start": 145.341,
   "end": 155.133,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "He confessed with a hang of the head. He collected acorns of every kind, he said, and the little silver one had been calling to him for months. He gave it back, and Agnes, who believes in second chances, still lets him deliver the milk. She has, however, moved the caddy to a higher shelf.",
   "start": 155.983,
   "end": 174.073,
   "pause": 0.0
  }
 ],
